{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And it shall be, if you heed, etc.</b> One must know the cutting point of the verse: if the statement is ״וּנְתָנְךָ״ etc., it should have said ״יִתֶּנְךָ HaShem,״ for His saying ״וּנְתָנְךָ״ implies an addition over what precedes. Or perhaps the cutting point of the verse is ״תִּשְׁמַע בְּקוֹל ה׳,״ and the intent in this is that through engagement in Torah one ascends in degrees to merit that HaShem speaks through him, which is His saying <b>you will hear the voice of HaShem your Elohim.</b> It will further be drawn for you from the hearing, to keep and to do — for the Torah shields from the sin alluded to in guarding — and also the power of Torah study is great, for it brings one to deed, which is His saying <b>and to do</b>; and further for you, ״HaShem will set you on high״ etc." "And apart from our way, the verse may intend to say that one must engage in Torah with the measure of their superiority over things that come from a supernal holy place, which is His saying <b>you will hear</b> — meaning, your hearing of them should be according to the stature of the One speaking them, who is HaShem your Elohim. And further, <b>to keep and to do</b>, after the manner of their statement, of blessed memory (Avot 4:5): הַלּוֹמֵד עַל מְנָת לְלַמֵּד מַסְפִּיקִין בְּיָדוֹ לִלְמוֹד וּלְלַמֵּד, and הַלּוֹמֵד עַל מְנָת לַעֲשׂוֹת and so forth until here — see how the choicest learning is in order to fulfill, which is His saying <b>if you will indeed heed</b> etc., meaning that you hear in order to keep and to do." "<b>And HaShem will set you on high, etc.</b> Behold, the Master apportioned the blessings' promises corresponding to the fulfillment of the commandments, each one according to what befits it. The totality of the commandments are three, inscribed in the verse ״אִם שָׁמוֹעַ תִּשְׁמַע״, and they are: Torah study, observance of negative commandments, and fulfillment of positive commandments. Corresponding to Torah study He promised the blessing ״HaShem will set you on high, and all these blessings shall come upon you and overtake you,״ and the meaning of the verse is that this corresponds to the reward of ״if you heed״ etc., in what He finished saying ״כִּי תִשְׁמַע בְּקוֹל ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ״ — see then that this promise corresponds to the reward of Torah study, and this too is directed toward it, for through the instrumentality of Torah they will be changed to be above all the nations who are not people of Torah." "And He said further, <b>and all these blessings shall come upon you</b> etc. — meaning, in addition to the promise ״HaShem will set you on high,״ he has yet further these blessings as reward for Torah. And His saying <b>and they shall overtake you</b> is to be explained after the manner of their statement, of blessed memory (Yalkut Reuveni, parashat Vayikra), that the Master, the Elohim of Israel — His measures are mercy; upon one who knocks on His mercy, HaShem fills his requests to pour goodness upon him as he desires, except that when that goodness descends below, the supernal Sanhedrin judges whether he is worthy; if he is not worthy, it does not reach him. Therefore He said ״and they shall overtake you״ — the blessings, for this is the essence of the promise, that they shall not be held back in the courts below." "<b>And behold,</b> you will find that all the blessings stated in the passage are directed corresponding to the reward of Torah study in this manner. He said <b>Blessed shall you be in the city</b> etc., after the manner of their statement, of blessed memory (Avodah Zarah 19b): דָּבָר זֶה כָּתוּב בַּתּוֹרָה וְשָׁנוּי בַּנְּבִיאִים וּמְשֻׁלָּשׁ בַּכְּתוּבִים — כָּל הָעוֹסֵק בְּתַלְמוּד תּוֹרָה נְכָסָיו מֻצְלָחִים." "And the verse's saying <b>בָּרוּךְ פְּרִי בִטְנְךָ</b> — go out and learn from Oved Edom the Gittite, into whose home the Ark of the Torah entered: how great was the fruit of his body in a short time, as they of blessed memory said (Berakhot 63a) that his wife and his eighteen daughters-in-law all gave birth at once, six in one womb:" "And the verse's saying <b>בָּרוּךְ אַתָּה בְּבוֹאֶךָ</b> etc. — that your departure from the world be without sin, etc. (Bava Metzi'a 107a), and this will come about through the agency of Torah study, which protects him from sin:" "And the verse's saying <b>יִתֵּן ה׳ אֶת אוֹיְבֶיךָ</b> etc., in the manner of the verse (II Chronicles 20:22): ״וּבְעֵת הֵחֵלּוּ בְרִנָּה נָתַן ה׳ מְאָרְבִים עַל בְּנֵי עַמּוֹן וְהַר שֵׂעִיר וְגוֹ׳ וַיִּנָּגֵפוּ״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Exodus Rabbah 23): there is no רִנָּה (song) but Torah." "And the verse's saying <b>יְצַו ה׳ אִתְּךָ אֶת הַבְּרָכָה</b> etc. — He also did not arrange this blessing above together with the bestowals of acquisitions of good things, where its place would properly be, and not after the defeat of the enemy. Rather, it comes to promise that there will not befall him what befell the subject of the miracle, against whom the prosecutors grew jealous because a new thing was done for him and the supernal beings served him in a manner contrary to nature. Go out and learn from the incident of Rava (Ta'anit 24b), who brought down rains out of their season, and similar cases. Therefore He placed it immediately after the statement <b>יִתֵּן ה׳ אֶת אוֹיְבֶיךָ</b> etc. — which is itself a miracle — <b>יְצַו ה׳ אִתְּךָ אֶת הַבְּרָכָה</b>, that the evil eye should not rule over it. He also said <b>וּבֵרַכְךָ בָּאָרֶץ</b> etc., meaning that the nations shall not gather against you to avenge the defeated ones, as Jacob said to his sons in the affair of Shechem (Genesis 34:30)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:19:58.479064517Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}